<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rouran Khaganate</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rouran</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rouran Khaganate was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nomadic-confederation">nomadic confederation</a> that dominated the Mongolian steppe from the late fourth to the mid-sixth century CE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Göktürks</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bumin-qaghan">Bumin</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashina-clan">Ashina clan</a> were originally its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, and their revolt destroyed Rouran power in the 550s. Accounts of the Gokturk Empire describe this rupture as the origin of the Göktürk state, while accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu Empire</a> list the Rouran among the steppe powers that inherited the Xiongnu military tradition.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Rouran were a tribal confederation that later became a state, and Chinese sources disagree about their ancestry. The Book of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> and Book of Liang connected them to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-wei">Book of Wei</a> traced them to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donghu">Donghu</a>, generally agreed to be Proto-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>. The state was multi-ethnic. According to the Chinese records, the founder of the khaganate, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yujiulu-shelun">Yujiulü Shelun</a>, descended from Mugulü, a slave of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a>. The Rouran remained politically fragmented until 402, when Shelun gained the support of all the Rouran chieftains and united them under one banner.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Immediately after unification the Rouran entered a long conflict with Northern Wei, beginning with a Wei offensive that drove them from the Ordos region. They expanded westward, defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiele">Tiele</a> and extended their territory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Roads</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a> were their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> until the beginning of the 5th century. The Rouran controlled trade routes and raided and subjugated oases such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaochang">Gaochang</a>. Fighting with Northern Wei continued, with Wei attacks in 429, 443, 449, 456 and 458, and marriage alliances such as that of 434. The Rouran also made marriage alliances with Eastern and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-wei">Western Wei</a>, each of which sought their support against the other.</p><p>In 551 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bumin-qaghan">Bumin</a> of the Ashina Göktürks quelled a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiele">Tiele</a> revolt for the Rouran and asked for a Rouran princess in return. The Rouran refused, saying he was only a blacksmith slave, and Bumin declared independence. He allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-wei">Western Wei</a> and attacked the Rouran in 552. After a defeat at Huaihuang the last great khan, Anagui, took his own life. In 555 the Göktürks under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muqan-qaghan">Muqan Qaghan</a> annihilated the Rouran, and reportedly all those handed over to the Turks, except children under sixteen, were killed.</p><p>Some scholars hold that Rouran remnants became the Pannonian Avars, who reached the Byzantine frontier in 567. Others contest this, and genetic studies of the Avar elite show a largely East Asian ancestry but cannot yet settle the relationship. The language of the Rouran is unknown: scholars have called it an isolate or a Mongolic language.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The rulers took the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a>, which later passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a>. Under Anagui the court adopted officials, advisers trained in the Chinese tradition and a staff of bodyguards, and its chief adviser was the Chinese Shunyu Tan. The Rouran first counted warriors with sheep droppings and later kept records on notched wood, and later adopted written Chinese for diplomacy. Burials in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> contain wooden-frame saddles and iron stirrups from the fourth and fifth centuries, among the earliest found in Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rouran Khaganate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Byzantine writers claimed they were fugitives from the Turks, and many historians link them with the <b>Rouran Khaganate of Mongolia</b>, destroyed by the Göktürks in the 550s; ancient DNA studies published in 2022 indicate that the early Avar elite had recent roots in northeastern Asia, which supports a…</blockquote><a href="/empire/avar-khaganate">Avar Khaganate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Bumin, leader of the Ashina clan and a vassal of the <b>Rouran Khaganate</b>, crushed a revolt of the Tiele tribes in 546 CE and asked for a Rouran princess as reward.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gokturk-empire">Gokturk Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>They were vassals of the <b>Rouran Khaganate</b> until the early 5th century, borrowing the title khan, before moving south-east to escape Rouran pressure.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalite Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
